A||||18||Brandon86SE||Getting out of a skid?????||||1||
Z||000000||Brandon86SE||11-29-2001||06:38 PM||||Well, I've now done 2 (unintentional  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) donuts in my car in the winter.  Once last year, and now once this year.  This morning on the way to school I hit an on-ramp that goes downhill, and turns at the same time.  Well, the rear-end started giving out, so I let off the glass, and turn into the skid.... nothing, give it gas, and continue turning into the skid..... skid more, decide I'm gonna spin (thank god there was nobody next to me), and start pumping the brakes until I'm done spinning.  (about 1.25 turns).  I ended up sideways, taking up both lanes of traffic, just under the crest of a hill, so oncoming traffic couldn't see me until they crested!!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">, so I nearly got hit by a couple, and stopped the rest, and was able to get back on the road and to/from school.  On the way home I filled up with gas as it was nearly empty, and gives me extra weight.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>So my questions is, how the heck to I recover from skids?????  I think I've done what I've been told, but no dice!  Any tips?  BTW, I have 60kilo's of sand in the front.  Thanks!<P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ferrari_cdn||11-29-2001||06:43 PM||||I've been told that it's nearly impossible to get out of a spin in a Fiero.  I've skidded in my car in the snow last night, and I realized that once the rear end starts to slip, GET it back under control immediately, by turning the wheels in the opposite direction, or you're gonna be in for a ride.<P>Then again, it was my first drive in the snow and dont have a whole lot of experience yet.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">   </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voytek||11-29-2001||06:44 PM||||Don't get into them, then you won't have to get out of them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Take your car to an empty parking lot at night and practice.  That's what I used to do with my Fiero.  In fact, I used to take it around some industrial streets where I work, at lunch and drive like I was at autocross.  It was fun.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pyric||11-29-2001||06:46 PM||||It kind of depends on if you're in a stick or an automatic.  In a manual transmission down shifting causes the engine to slow you down.<P>And you can always use the emergency brake... that is if yours works  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Brandon86SE||11-29-2001||06:47 PM||||Yes, it's a 4 speed, and my e-brake is good, I just got the cables redone.  I've been mostly engine braking lately (downshifting), but the problem is that you have to get it going real slow first, or else you drop too much torque/horse to the wheels, and only makes things worse in a skid.<P>Brandon||||reg||1
Z||000005||Posthumane||11-29-2001||10:03 PM||||Hey brandon, where are you at? maybe we could go to an open lot and practice skid control together. Email me at jpankows@ucalgary.ca if your interested. BTW, where did you spin?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||11-29-2001||10:14 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> Downshifting in a spin!??!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>1) NEVER get off the gas. Drags the back end out<BR>2) DON'T HAMMER the gas, either<BR>3) Moderate throttle, keep it steady, and turn the wheel where you want to go. <BR>4) DO NOT OVERCORRECT. Figure out how much wheel-turn you need, and CUT IN HALF.<P>Mid-engine makes for nice fast turns, but it makes your ass a little heavy... In a full-blown spin, LOCK the tires until you slow down the spin. Then point the wheels straight to the road... Then give it some gas. That should pull you out. The problem with this is that it takes time. MAYBE not enough before hitting that tree  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><BR>This I learned from Auto-crossing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||BlackStang95||11-29-2001||10:36 PM||||Rear wheel drive in general is a nightmare and a half in snow.  Stick makes it even tougher. If another vehicle isnt available try winter tires, or at least snow chains if they are legal.  Try and keep your speed and revs low.  I think some weight like sand in the back of the car might help too, not sure though, i know it does in the stang.  Hope this helps.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Neal||11-29-2001||10:48 PM||||Brandon, SNOW TIRES, even on ice my fiero would stick like glue (the fiero is the WORST dounut car ive owned with the winter tires).  Yokahama GuardX get my recomendation, also get em SKINNY, im using 185's.  I wouldnt hesitate 195's but i wouldnt go wider, also skinny = cheap<P>Blackstang, in fieros you might wanna add weight to the front cuz were already rear heavy. but i wouldnt worry about it.  Fieros make GOOD winter cars if they have good tires.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||11-29-2001||10:56 PM||||You'll notice that there is PLENTY of room up front for said sand-bags  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I'm loading up next thursday when I buy my snow tires  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000010||soup||11-29-2001||10:58 PM||||What I do seeing as how im jsut got my Fiero and am still getting used to it, I purposely take the car out where there is no traffic and purposely get into minor spins, at first I would usually end up sideways but doing it about 5 times you find out how to correct it.. also as said as above do not overcorrect, look down the road and focus on something, steer towards it, dont try and correct the steering, never downshift and never use the ebrake in a skid, use the ebrake if you want to skid...||||reg||1
Z||000011||California Kid||11-29-2001||11:05 PM||||Brandon,  Mach10 has given you some very sound advise, try practicing in an empty slick parking lot. It's easy to learn what the limits are and you'll find it requires quick reaction time to stay out of trouble if you've pushed it too far.<P>Do Not, I repeat Do Not add any weight to the rear of your Fiero!! I don't want to read about you parked up against some tree. You can add 50 to 75 lbs in the front compartment for winter, which will help your front tires grip a little more, and reduce those nasty hydro planes over snow sluch rut ridges.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Patrick's Dad||11-29-2001||11:38 PM||||I did some Olympics worthy spins in my 2M4, until the time I realized what it meant to have a rear end heavy car.<P>When the rear tires let go, the weighted end of the car wants to go first. I have learned to let it; using the steering wheels as "rudders." Believe me, on Rt128 in MA, you don't want to be spinning along all four lanes, so I just guided the car off into the breakdown lane. Although I was pointed in the wrong direction, my car had sustained no damage, and I was able to breathe through the adrenaline rush. When there was an opening, I spun the car around, and continued on my way.<P>Rear drivers, in general, have difficulties in accelerating in snow, because the rear end wants to swing around under power. When you slow the wheels down, inertia will continue this in a mid engine car, but weight will take over in a front engined car, and the rear end will swing back into line. I had a 280ZX that I had to deal with getting up an icy hill. After several attempts, I turned it around and successfully backed up the hill.<P>Remember that no vehicle outbrakes another in snow/ice given similar tires. Of course, there are those drivers that think otherwise, and you can frequently see thier tall boxes upside down on the median....||||reg||1
Z||000013||BlackStang95||11-29-2001||11:41 PM||||With the bad differential finding traction is extremely difficult even in the rain.  If i let the clutch out at over 1700 rpms they'll start to spin.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mach10||11-29-2001||11:53 PM||||Did your car come with a stock limited slip? Doesn't sound like a bad diff making life hard... More like an open diff with one wheel slipping  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000015||Mach10||11-30-2001||12:00 AM||||I think that's what BS95 meant... Putting weight in the rear of a stang makes a HUGE difference, for the better. ||||reg||10
Z||000016||Ayrow||11-30-2001||12:57 AM||||I know all about this!!!!!<P>I wrecked a brand-new '88 Formula on the interstate, in the snow, on X-mas morning doing this!!<P>Worst day of my life!!!<P>Since then I have spent a lot of time racing different sized cars and have learned how to handle it properly.<P>One has to understand that the Fiero is a different animal when it comes to recovering a loss of control.<BR>The weight distribution is almost 50/50, so the handling aspects of the car are very good.<BR>However, once you lose control, the car will act much differently than a front engine car.<BR>Having the engine in the back makes the car spin easily, but predictably.<BR>If you are past the point-of-no-return and you are spinning like crazy, the best thing you can do it let the wheels spin freely (ie. put it in neutral) and hold the steering wheel straight ahead.  If all goes well, the car should straighten out going in the same general direction you were traveling.  This could be bad or good, but will more often than not, give you the ability to gain enough control to brake or steer again.<BR>This is especially true on ice or snow - you are not going to "power" your way back into a straight line on these surfaces.<P>Practicing on a rain-drenched parking lot is good advice and a good way to learn.<P>Good Luck!<P>Ayrow||||reg||1
Z||000017||crzyone||11-30-2001||01:15 AM||||Hey Brandon, winter tires are really good for snow, if you'd like I have a brand new set on an old set of 85 GT rims, they work well and there for sale.  <P>------------------<BR>86 GT 4 speed<BR>85 SC 5 speed||||reg||1
Z||000018||Brandon86SE||11-30-2001||01:24 AM||||crzyone - well, how much are you asking, and what kind of tread is left on 'em?  PM me, thanks.<P>Pusthumane - sounds good to me, I'll get back to you once I get the car running again.  My alt went on me today, and my baby's stranded at Chinook Center.  Just a bad, bad day.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I spun out on the Bow Bottom Trail on-ramp to Deerfoot.<P>I live in the SE, just off Bow Bottom Trail BTW<P>Brandon||||reg||1
